I read the diff and the surrounding precond/orchestrator code. I did not run the tests or ESBMC. No finding is a verified crash, and finding 4 is a guess about ESBMC's output. I checked out the PR locally to read it, then switched back to `main`. Findings, most severe first:

```json
[
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/core/invariant.py",
    "line": 316,
    "summary": "discharge_invariant_source drops the user's --timeout and passthrough esbmc flags when listing units and callers. Verification honours them, so the caller set is computed from a different translation unit than the one verified.",
    "failure_scenario": "`forseti invariant-discharge f.c --type T -t 300 -- -DFEATURE -Iinc`. `discharge_invariants` calls `analyze_translation_unit(source, esbmc_bin=..., timeout_s=<default 30.0>)` with no `extra_flags`. The `-D`/`-I` flags reach only the verify port. With `#ifdef FEATURE` callers, the clang parse either fails (ListUnitsError, exit 1) or lists a different function and caller set than the code actually verified. A DISCHARGED can then rest on callers that are not the ones compiled. The slow-parse timeout is also not user-controllable. `discharge_invariants` types `timeout_s` as `float` while the Core signature allows `None`."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/precond/invariant_discharge.py",
    "line": 531,
    "summary": "The obligation is asserted by dereferencing the pointer parameter at callee entry, before any NULL guard the callee has. A caller that deliberately passes NULL to a NULL-tolerant callee fails with a dereference failure instead of an invariant violation.",
    "failure_scenario": "`static int rb_push(ring_t *r, int v) { if (!r) return -1; ... }` and a closed `test_null()` that calls `rb_push(NULL, 1)`. The injected `{ const ring_t *self = r; __ESBMC_assert((self->count <= 4), ...); }` runs first and dereferences NULL. The Violated trace carries no entry label, so it is classified CALLER_VIOLATED and then UNKNOWN. A correct caller makes the whole run exit 2, and the discharge for that callee can never succeed. Guard the assertion (`!self || (expr)`), or explicitly document that NULL-passing callers are unsupported."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/precond/invariant_discharge.py",
    "line": 819,
    "summary": "The inline re-entry handling re-implements `discharge._attributed` and the `_self_reachable` logic. It also imports private helpers across modules (`_self_reachable`, `_line_directive`).",
    "failure_scenario": "`discharge.py` already has `_attributed(checks, function, reentrant=...)`, which does the same OBLIGATION_VIOLATED -> UNATTRIBUTED rewrite, including the `check.caller != function` guard. The new copy has a different message and no guard. A later fix to the S3 attribution rule (see the #166 rationale) will not reach the invariant path. Promote the helpers to public names or a shared module and call them."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/precond/invariant_discharge.py",
    "line": 795,
    "summary": "Any assuming function that another T * function calls (including via a relay such as `rb_push` calling `rb_len(self)`) can never be discharged. Its only referrer takes parameters, so it is UNCHECKED, so the status is always INCOMPLETE.",
    "failure_scenario": "In a typical ring buffer, `rb_push`/`rb_pop` call static helpers like `rb_full(r)` or `rb_len(r)`. Each helper's referrers include a parameter-taking method, which yields `UNCHECKED` and permanent INCOMPLETE for that helper. Obligations are injected into the helper but the real constructing caller (`main`) reaches it only transitively, so it is never checked. A violation hidden behind a relay is never reported as violated, only as 'assumed'. Whole-run outcome degrades to `incomplete`, and exit 0 gives no signal."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/precond/invariant_discharge.py",
    "line": 499,
    "summary": "Exit code 0 for outcome `empty` (and `incomplete`) makes a typo'd `--type`, a missing store, or an unsubmitted invariant look like success in scripts and CI.",
    "failure_scenario": "`forseti invariant-discharge f.c --type rign_t` (typo), or a wrong `--store-root`, finds no invariant or no assuming function. `discharge_invariants` returns `()` and the run is `empty`, which maps to exit 0 with only a prose line. A pipeline gating on the exit code treats 'nothing was checked' as a pass. The repo guidance is that fail-closed states must not silently pass. `empty` should exit non-zero, or at least be distinguishable via a dedicated code."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/core/cli.py",
    "line": 686,
    "summary": "Adding a `with_max_len` flag to the shared `_add_check_phase_arguments` leaves its docstring stale. It still says the helper registers `--max-len`. The `--unwind-ladder` help still describes the shared default.",
    "failure_scenario": "The docstring says it registers `-k/--unwind`, `--unwind-ladder`, `-t/--timeout`, `--max-len`, `--json` and describes `--max-len`. For `invariant-discharge` it now silently omits `--max-len`, so the docs mislead maintainers. A special-case boolean on shared infrastructure invites further per-subcommand flags. Splitting the helper into a max-len-free base plus a small add-on would be cleaner."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/precond/invariant_discharge.py",
    "line": 846,
    "summary": "The work is repeated per (invariant, callee). Every combination re-writes the obligation and site copies and re-runs every caller, plus a probe, from scratch. The same obligation text is re-derived for each caller.",
    "failure_scenario": "A type with N stored invariants and M assuming functions runs roughly N*M*(callers*2) ESBMC invocations. Invariants cannot be combined into one conjunction here, so a large project pays for each. The site probe does not depend on the invariant expression, yet `_check_caller` re-runs it for every property. Cache the site-probe result per (callee, caller, k) at least, and the per-callee `open_callers_fn` call."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/forseti/precond/invariant_discharge.py",
    "line": 626,
    "summary": "A caller that violates a property of its own (for example an out-of-bounds access) is mapped to UNKNOWN, which exits 2. The run stays UNKNOWN rather than VIOLATED for a genuine, concrete caller fault.",
    "failure_scenario": "`main` has a real buffer overflow before the call to the callee. The Violated result has no entry label, giving CALLER_VIOLATED with an `esbmc_result`, and `_inconclusive` then returns True, so the status is UNKNOWN ('inconclusive'). Yet ESBMC produced a concrete counterexample. Nothing was learned about the invariant, but the run reports 'unknown' and hides a real defect behind a bound/timeout-style status. The misleading label could cause a user to raise k pointlessly."
  }
]
```
